WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.740
A 3,200-pound NASA spacecraft is expected to re-enter Earth's atmosphere within hours. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 243. A White House Space Proclamation, Europe's Harris spacecraft at a binary asteroid, and a student-built lunar landing pad come next, with cosmic spotlight later. The United States Space Force, according to spaceflight now, now predicts re-entry between 1142 and 1342 Greenwich Mean Time today. That forecast

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:32.180
as covers a band lying mostly over ocean,

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:35.700
staying within 21 degrees north or south of the equator.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.100
The Neil Garell's swift observatory has operated

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:40.340
for more than two decades.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.940
Those years went to X-ray and ultraviolet observations

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:46.980
of gamma ray bursts and other transient events.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.980
As of the latest update, the spacecraft could fall

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:51.320
to Earth as soon as tonight.

00:00:52.307 --> 00:00:55.207
A presidential proclamation reported by NASA Watch

00:00:55.207 --> 00:00:57.967
declares that the solar system and all outer space

00:00:57.967 --> 00:01:04.147
beyond Earth are open to exploration, development, use, and permanent presence by American citizens.

00:01:04.507 --> 00:01:10.247
The tech site's Constitutional Authority and references Artemis II and the planned Artemis III

00:01:10.247 --> 00:01:16.067
missions. It notes the April success of Artemis II and describes Artemis III as one of the most

00:01:16.067 --> 00:01:21.887
complex test flight campaigns in the nation's space program. The document sets a goal of returning

00:01:21.887 --> 00:01:25.547
to the moon by 2028 and sending an American to Mars.

00:01:26.027 --> 00:01:30.567
It reaffirms that the United States will lead in building facilities and establishing a

00:01:30.567 --> 00:01:33.187
permanent presence to extend American exploration.

00:01:34.206 --> 00:01:39.806
Europe's Harris spacecraft, according to Space.com, has approached the binary asteroid system

00:01:39.806 --> 00:01:41.846
close enough to distinguish the two bodies.

00:01:42.426 --> 00:01:47.886
The images show the smaller dimorphos, the target of NASA's earlier dark deflection test,

00:01:48.306 --> 00:01:49.426
resolved from Didymos.

00:01:49.426 --> 00:01:53.966
The probe can now resolve the pair as separate objects in its approach trajectory.

00:01:54.989 --> 00:01:59.689
University of Alabama in Huntsville students and researchers, according to Universe Today,

00:02:00.309 --> 00:02:04.169
conducted three rocket motor firing tests on a prototype pad this summer.

00:02:04.669 --> 00:02:08.689
The design captured and redirected exhaust through an integrated vent system.

00:02:09.269 --> 00:02:13.949
The prototype demonstrated the ability to manage rocket exhaust without damaging the surrounding

00:02:13.949 --> 00:02:14.409
surface.

00:02:14.409 --> 00:02:19.309
The test marked progress towards safe landing infrastructure for future moon bases.

00:02:19.689 --> 00:02:25.536
The Hubble Space Telescope, according to Space.com, remains healthy but faces increasing

00:02:25.536 --> 00:02:26.636
atmospheric drag.

00:02:27.136 --> 00:02:31.636
NASA is asking companies for concepts that could raise its orbit and prolong its science

00:02:31.636 --> 00:02:32.056
mission.

00:02:32.616 --> 00:02:37.236
The request seeks options to counteract the gradual decay caused by residual atmosphere

00:02:37.236 --> 00:02:38.336
at Hubble's altitude.

00:02:39.397 --> 00:02:42.937
A rover on Mars can have only minutes to judge a boulder in its path.

00:02:42.937 --> 00:02:47.377
A loose chunk the size of a suitcase or a buried slab as big as a truck.

00:02:47.817 --> 00:02:52.037
That difference decides whether the machine spends hours chipping at stone that will not

00:02:52.037 --> 00:02:54.557
move or turns aside and drives around.

00:02:55.097 --> 00:02:59.837
On Earth, a person settles that with a glance and a kick, but a rover on another world

00:02:59.837 --> 00:03:00.977
has none of that feel.

00:03:01.557 --> 00:03:06.277
Virginia Tech engineers, according to Fizz.org, are teaching machines to listen for the

00:03:06.277 --> 00:03:06.977
answer instead.

00:03:07.577 --> 00:03:11.317
The method begins with a tap on whatever rock still sticks out of the ground.

00:03:11.317 --> 00:03:17.337
A hammer fitted with sensors strikes that exposed face, and a scanning laser-dopler vibrometer

00:03:17.337 --> 00:03:21.117
records how the vibration runs through the buried mass and comes back out.

00:03:21.777 --> 00:03:27.037
To train the system, the team planted 18 concrete blocks in sand, each a different size

00:03:27.037 --> 00:03:28.377
and set it a different depth.

00:03:29.017 --> 00:03:32.397
They measured vibration at several points on every exposed surface.

00:03:32.997 --> 00:03:37.677
Those frequency patterns, together with the visible geometry of each block, went into

00:03:37.677 --> 00:03:40.037
a neural network built with just two hidden layers.

00:03:40.037 --> 00:03:40.137
years.

00:03:40.437 --> 00:03:45.157
Once that network was trained, it could estimate burial depth from a fresh set of vibration

00:03:45.157 --> 00:03:45.657
readings.

00:03:46.197 --> 00:03:51.997
Luz Sand and Solid Rock carry vibration differently, so a buried boulder sends back a signature that

00:03:51.997 --> 00:03:53.817
a pebble sitting on the surface does not.

00:03:54.517 --> 00:03:59.637
On Mars or the Moon, a robotic excavator could use that contrast to stop wasting work on

00:03:59.637 --> 00:04:00.777
a rocket cannot move.

00:04:01.617 --> 00:04:05.437
Eric Kamenderra led the project with Ph.D. candidate Jan Ruan.

00:04:06.177 --> 00:04:12.857
Commentara is an assistant professor of mechanical engineering who previously worked as an engineer at NASA Langley Research Center.

00:04:13.237 --> 00:04:16.117
The results appear in the IAPE sensors journal.

00:04:16.737 --> 00:04:21.437
Soil stiffness, contact angle, and rock shape all change the signal that comes back.

00:04:21.917 --> 00:04:28.237
Beryl Depth is still hard to read because the feedback depends on the rock's geometry and on how the soil grips it.

00:04:28.237 --> 00:04:33.737
The team selected features from the vibration data and from the exposed shape, and that selection

00:04:33.737 --> 00:04:36.477
produce reliable estimates even with the small network.

00:04:37.057 --> 00:04:41.037
For now they plan to keep building data sets and to keep testing the method on Earth.

00:04:41.597 --> 00:04:47.077
The same tools could assess unstable rock formations, measure underground caverns, or help

00:04:47.077 --> 00:04:49.617
after disasters that leave hazards under debris.

00:04:50.217 --> 00:04:55.517
How those models will behave in Martian Regolith is still open, because grain size, moisture

00:04:55.517 --> 00:05:01.377
temperature there are only partly known. Tests on analog terrain will decide whether a laboratory

00:05:01.377 --> 00:05:07.437
sandbox is enough, or whether the method can work on a real planetary surface. Researchers

00:05:07.437 --> 00:05:12.757
propose that finding extraterrestrial signals requires not only the right direction, but also

00:05:12.757 --> 00:05:17.657
the right timing. The approach applies game theory to model mutual expectations between

00:05:17.657 --> 00:05:23.237
civilizations about when to transmit or receive. Successful detection depends on coordinated

00:05:23.237 --> 00:05:26.917
associated assumptions about transmission schedules across interstellar distances.

00:05:27.918 --> 00:05:32.938
Frequent collisions between stars and the early universe, according to Space.com, could

00:05:32.938 --> 00:05:35.578
have allowed rapid growth of supermassive black holes.

00:05:36.198 --> 00:05:41.198
The same process may account for the mysterious little red dots observed by the James Webb Space

00:05:41.198 --> 00:05:41.718
Telescope.

00:05:42.338 --> 00:05:46.498
The collisions would have provided a mechanism for black holes to reach enormous masses

00:05:46.498 --> 00:05:48.218
on short cosmic time scales.

00:05:48.218 --> 00:05:55.030
New simulations reported by Fizz.org show that for irregular asteroids, the precise location

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:59.370
where x-rays from a nuclear blast strike can significantly affect the resulting trajectory

00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:00.070
change.

00:06:00.650 --> 00:06:05.130
Lumpy surfaces create uneven momentum transfer that must be accounted for in deflection

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:05.650
planning.

00:06:06.434 --> 00:06:12.154
An object nickname Kapotaro appeared in early James Webb images as a possible early galaxy.

00:06:12.894 --> 00:06:18.374
Images taken 3.5 years apart showed the faint red source had shifted position, identifying

00:06:18.374 --> 00:06:20.534
identifying it instead as a cold brown dwarf.

00:06:21.174 --> 00:06:25.814
The motion revealed that the object is nearby rather than a distant high-red shift galaxy.

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:31.980
Airbus, according to Space News, has developed a device called Detumpler designed to stop

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:36.040
dead satellites from tumbling, reducing collision risk in crowded orbits.

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:40.760
It offers a lightweight solution for satellites that fail before a controlled deorbit.

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:46.180
The device provides passive spin stabilization without requiring active control systems.

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:52.771
Three United States astronauts aboard the International Space Station, or ISS, cast mid-term

00:06:52.771 --> 00:06:56.151
ballots electronically from orbit, according to space.com.

00:06:56.531 --> 00:07:01.271
The crew used secure transmission through NASA's tracking and data relay satellite system

00:07:01.271 --> 00:07:03.491
to reach county election officials in Texas.

00:07:04.291 --> 00:07:09.771
Americans have voted from space since 1997 under a Texas law that first enabled David

00:07:09.771 --> 00:07:11.091
Wolf to vote from Meir.

00:07:11.511 --> 00:07:16.191
The process routes the encrypted ballot from the station through White Sands test facility

00:07:16.191 --> 00:07:21.511
in New Mexico. From White Sands, it goes to the Mission Control Center at Johnson Space Center

00:07:21.511 --> 00:07:27.391
before final delivery to the county clerk. A Neil Menon, Luke Delaney, and Jessica Watkins

00:07:27.391 --> 00:07:32.771
each completed an electronic form inside their station compartments. They sent those forms to

00:07:32.771 --> 00:07:39.333
Harris County. NASA's Artemis IV Lunar Mission, according to Space.com, will require an unknown

00:07:39.333 --> 00:07:45.133
number of starship tanker flights in two separate orbits. The plan involves multiple rendezvouses

00:07:45.133 --> 00:07:48.353
and propellant transfers before the lander can descend to the surface.

00:07:48.973 --> 00:07:53.773
The architecture demands precise coordination between tanker and lander vehicles in different

00:07:53.773 --> 00:07:54.693
orbital regimes.

00:07:55.812 --> 00:08:01.352
In brief from around the field, Thailand, according to CNA, is expanding its satellite programs

00:08:01.352 --> 00:08:05.412
